From b7f6d9806ca0ee90518098f7adc2b310d8465ec3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oseltamivir <bryan@semianalysis.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 16:20:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] [Model][Engram] Disk-backed Engram n-gram tables for
 DeepSeek-V4.1

Stacked on #56512, whose prefetch and DP sharding this needs: the disk path
has real latency to hide, and the same `_allocate_weights` / `_storage` hooks
that PR introduces are where a file-backed shard belongs.

DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash's Engram table is 23.60 GiB per rank per Engram layer
(96M rows x 256, fp8 + ue8m0 scales). Two Engram layers at TP4 is ~189 GiB of
pinned host RAM, and that -- not GPU memory -- is what decides whether the
model fits a given host. The table is a pure gather, so its working set is
tiny next to its size, which is the argument #54070 makes for the Qwen4Exp
PLE table. This is that change applied to Engram.

`EngramConfig.disk_offload_dir` (default `VLLM_ENGRAM_DISK_OFFLOAD_DIR`) maps
each shard from `<dir>/engram_r<shard>.{weight,scale}.bin`. First boot streams
the checkpoint through a read-write mapping, so a host with less free RAM than
the table can still load it; later boots map the finished file and skip the
checkpoint read. A sidecar records the shard geometry so a half-written file,
or one left by a different TP/EDP layout, is rebuilt rather than silently
gathered from. It is an alternative to dp_shared_memory, not a companion, and
both require cpu_offload.

Engram differs from PLE in the part that matters. PLE gathers rows in a
separate offload worker on the host; Engram's lookup is a Triton kernel
dereferencing a UVA pointer into pinned memory. A mapped file cannot be read
that way -- UVA needs page-locked memory, and pinning the mapping would pull
the table back into RAM and defeat the change. So the disk path gathers rows
on the host and dequantizes only the gathered rows on device, through a new
`_engram_dequant_rows_kernel` mirroring the existing kernel's index and ue8m0
math. Rows are deduplicated first: a batch repeats n-grams, and the distinct
row count is what the disk actually serves.

The UVA and shared-memory paths are untouched and stay the default.

