From 0850b0323c0e1ec675b78705aa1fd956f8601a98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oseltamivir <bryan@semianalysis.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 16:31:11 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] [Model][Engram] Prefetch disk lookups off the forward
 thread

#56512's prefetch runs the lookup on a side CUDA stream, which is the right
shape for a UVA table: the gather is device work, so another stream overlaps
it. A file-backed shard is not. Its gather blocks on a device-to-host copy of
the hash ids and then on the filesystem, both on the calling thread, so a side
stream overlaps nothing and every Engram layer stalls on disk in turn.

Split the disk lookup into its host and device halves. `stage_disk_rows`
resolves the rows and reads them into pinned memory; `finish_disk_rows` copies
them over and dequantizes. `prepare_embeddings` submits the host half to a
single-worker pool at forward entry, so it runs while the decoder layers
before this one execute, and `_ready_rows` waits for it before the device half
goes onto the prefetch stream.

One worker per layer, so gathers stay ordered and cannot outrun the single
staging buffer a layer owns. The worker sets the device explicitly: a fresh
thread starts on device 0, which is the wrong one on every rank but the first.

